<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG-ONLY manifest additions. Merged by AGP into the debug variant
  only; the release APK is byte-for-byte unaffected.

  QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES exists here for ONE reason: DisplayCapabilityProbe
  enumerates installed packages to (a) identify which package holds
  DEVICE OWNER, and (b) list candidate vendor display-control apps. From
  Android 11 (API 30) up, `getInstalledPackages` is filtered to whatever
  the manifest `<queries>` block declares unless the app holds this
  permission — so on a release build the probe sees a handful of packages
  and cannot tell "no vendor control app installed" from "not allowed to
  look".

  Why debug-only rather than shipping it in release:
    - It is a broad visibility grant on an app that has just been through
      a security remediation program. The probe is a bench/diagnostic
      tool; the fleet does not need it to run the player.
    - Google Play treats QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES as a restricted permission.
      We sideload today, but keeping it out of release means the release
      APK never has to argue that case.
    - The probe degrades honestly without it: its `vendorPackages.
      enumerable` field reports false, so a short candidate list is never
      mistaken for an empty one.

  The release build still reports device-owner state correctly for the
  packages that matter — `DevicePolicyManager.isDeviceOwnerApp()` accepts
  any package name and the Manager companion is declared in the main
  manifest's <queries> block.

  So: run the DEBUG build on the Goodview bench unit for discovery, run
  release everywhere else.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES" />

</manifest>
